# Functions and Modularity: Reusable, Readable Code

> A function gives a thought a name. This lesson turns scattered
> scripts into structured programs: defining functions, passing data
> through parameters, understanding scope, and organising code into
> modules and packages you can reuse for years.

## 1. Why Functions Exist: Decomposition and DRY

Two forces shape every codebase. **Decomposition** says: break the
problem into small, nameable pieces. **DRY** — *Don't Repeat
Yourself* — says: every piece of knowledge should live in exactly one
place. Functions serve both: they name an operation once and let
every caller share the same, tested implementation.

| Without functions | With functions |
| --- | --- |
| The same six lines copied in ten places | One function, ten calls |
| A bug is fixed ten times (or nine, and forgotten) | Fix once, every caller benefits |
| One endless scroll of code | Code skimmed by meaningful names |

The payoff is measurable: short, single-purpose functions are easier
to test, easier to name, and measurably less defect-prone than long
ones.

@chart bar title="Defect density vs function length (illustrative defects per KLOC)" labels:
Under 10 lines
10-20 lines
20-40 lines
Over 40 lines

data:
1.0
1.7
2.6
4.3
@/chart

## 2. def, return, and the Shape of a Function

```python
def bmi(weight_kg, height_m):
    """Return body mass index rounded to one decimal place."""
    if height_m <= 0:
        raise ValueError("height must be positive")
    return round(weight_kg / height_m ** 2, 1)

print(bmi(60, 1.65))     # 22.0
```

A `def` statement creates a function object and binds a name to it;
the body runs only when called. **`return`** sends a value back *and
exits immediately*. A function with no `return` (or a bare `return`)
returns `None` — the classic cause of "my function prints the answer,
but the variable is None".

[definition]
A **pure function** depends only on its inputs and returns a value
without touching anything outside itself. Pure functions are trivial
to test and safe to run in parallel; impure ones (printing, writing
files, editing globals) belong at the edges of a program.
[/definition]

## 3. Parameters vs Arguments

**Parameters** are the names in the definition; **arguments** are the
values in the call — the recipe lists ingredients, the kitchen
receives the food.

```python
def greet(name, punctuation="!"):     # parameters
    return f"Hello, {name}{punctuation}"

greet("Ada")                          # positional argument
greet(punctuation="?", name="Ada")    # keyword arguments, any order
greet("Ada", punctuation="!!")        # mixed: positional first, always
```

Rules worth memorising: positional arguments come before keyword
arguments; a parameter with a default may be omitted; parameters
without defaults are mandatory. Defaults are evaluated **once**, at
definition time — remember the mutable-default trap.

[note]
A function should do one job, and its name should say the job. If
you cannot name it precisely — `process_data` — it is probably two
functions wearing one coat.
[/note]

## 4. *args and **kwargs: Variable Signatures

Sometimes a function must accept *any* number of inputs — a logger,
a formatter, a wrapper. `*args` collects extra positional arguments
into a tuple; `**kwargs` collects extra keyword arguments into a
dict.

```python
def report(title, *scores, **labels):
    print(title, scores, labels)

report("Quiz", 8, 9, 7, topic="loops", total=10)
# Quiz (8, 9, 7) {'topic': 'loops', 'total': 10}
```

The same stars work in the *opposite* direction — unpacking:

```python
point = (3, 4)
distance = math.hypot(*point)          # hypot(3, 4)
config = {"sep": " | "}
print("a", "b", **config)              # print("a", "b", sep=" | ")
```

[tip]
Conventional parameter order: `def f(positional, default=1, *args,
keyword_only, **kwargs)`. Anything after `*args` must be passed by
keyword — a cheap way to force readable calls at the cost of one
comma.
[/tip]

## 5. Scope: The LEGB Rule

When a name is looked up, Python searches four scopes **in order**:
**L**ocal (inside the current function), **E**nclosing (an outer
function), **G**lobal (module level), **B**uilt-in (`len`, `print`).
First hit wins — which is why an inner variable can *shadow* an
outer one.

```python
rate = 0.9                       # Global

def outer():
    rate = 0.8                   # Enclosing
    def inner():
        rate = 0.7               # Local — wins the lookup
        return rate
    return inner()

print(outer())                   # 0.7
```

Assigning to a name *creates a local variable* unless you declare
otherwise. `global x` rebinds a module-level name; `nonlocal x`
rebinds one in the enclosing function. Reading follows LEGB; writing
stays local by default.

[warning]
Functions that read and write globals are hard to test and reason
about — any caller's result depends on hidden state set elsewhere.
Pass what a function needs as parameters and return what it
produces; treat `global` as a code smell, not a feature.
[/warning]

## 6. Docstrings and Type Hints

A **docstring** is a string literal on the first line of a function —
stored in `__doc__`, shown by `help()`, read by editors and
documentation tools.

```python
def moving_average(values, window):
    """Return the average of each `window`-sized slice.

    >>> moving_average([1, 2, 3, 4], 2)
    [1.5, 2.5, 3.5]
    """
    return [sum(values[i:i + window]) / window
            for i in range(len(values) - window + 1)]
```

Type hints add machine-checkable declarations:
`def bmi(weight_kg: float, height_m: float) -> float:`. Python
ignores them at run time, but tools such as `mypy` catch whole
categories of bugs before the program ever runs.

| Tool | Reads | Gives you |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Docstring | `help(f)`, IDE pop-ups, doctests | Purpose, usage, runnable examples |
| Type hints | `mypy`, IDE completion | Checked calls, fewer type bugs |

## 7. Modules and Import Strategies

Any `.py` file is a **module**: `utils.py` defines module `utils`.
`import` runs the file once and binds a namespace — imports are also
how you reach the standard library (`math`, `json`, `random`) and
the wider ecosystem.

```python
import math                       # math.sqrt(2)
from datetime import date          # date.today() — direct name
import numpy as np                 # conventional alias
from os import path as p           # precise and aliased
```

| Style | Call looks like | Use when |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `import module` | `module.func()` | Default choice — origin is explicit |
| `from module import func` | `func()` | A few well-known names |
| `from module import *` | `func()` | Almost never — pollutes the namespace |
| `import module as m` | `m.func()` | Community aliases (`np`, `pd`) |

[important]
`from x import *` can silently overwrite your own names with the
module's — a bug that appears only when a name collides. Explicit
imports keep every call site honest about where a name comes from.
[/important]

## 8. Packages and Program Composition

A **package** is a directory of modules with an `__init__.py`,
enabling dotted imports: `myapp.io.readers`. Packages are how a
program stops being one file and becomes an architecture.

@diagram tree id="package-layout" layout="tree" aria="A small project laid out as a package with core and io subpackages and tests"
  "myapp/" -> "__init__.py"
  "myapp/" -> "core/"
  "myapp/" -> "io/"
  "myapp/" -> "tests/"
  "core/" -> "utils.py"
  "core/" -> "models.py"
  "io/" -> "readers.py"
@/diagram

```python
# myapp/core/utils.py
def clamp(value, low, high):
    """Return value forced into the range low..high."""
    return max(low, min(value, high))

# anywhere else in the project
from myapp.core.utils import clamp
```

Good programs follow **composition**: functions calling functions and
objects built from other objects — small parts stacked, rather than
deep inheritance trees or one giant `main`. Keep each module about
one job, keep the import graph free of cycles, and guard the entry
point with `if __name__ == "__main__":` so a module can run as a
script *and* be imported safely.

## 9. Quick Revision

| Concept | One-line recall |
| --- | --- |
| DRY | One definition, many callers — fix bugs once |
| Parameters vs arguments | Names in `def` vs values in the call |
| `*args` / `**kwargs` | Tuple of extra positionals / dict of keyword extras |
| LEGB | Local, Enclosing, Global, Built-in — first match wins |
| Docstring | First-line string read by `help()` — write it always |
| Package | Directory with `__init__.py`, imported with dots |
